{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 32, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond of Antioch was a nobleman from the Crusader states in the Levant He was the count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Tripoli He was born around 1140 and died on 29 June 1187 at the Battle of Hattin\n\n*Early life and reign*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1140 and was the only son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Tripoli He had two sisters Melisende and Lucia He succeeded his father as count of Tripoli in 1152 when he was around 12 years old During his reign he had to deal with the aggressive expansion of the Zengid dynasty which controlled Aleppo and Mosul He also had to navigate the complex political landscape of the Crusader states which included the Kingdom of Jerusalem the County of Edessa and the Principality of Antioch\n\n*Relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem*\n\nRaymond III had a close relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and its rulers He was married twice to princesses of the Kingdom of Jerusalem His first wife was Eschiva of Bethsan the daughter of Walter of Bethsan and Maria Comnena His second wife was Eschiva of Saint Omer the daughter of William of Saint Omer and Agnes of Courtenay He also had a close relationship with King Amalric I of Jerusalem and helped him in his campaigns against the Zengids He was also present at the Siege of Ascalon in 1153 which marked the end of the Kingdom of Jerusalem's conquests in Egypt\n\n*Battle of Hattin and death*\n\nRaymond III is perhaps best known for his role in the Battle of Hattin which took place on 4 July 1187 He commanded the left wing of the Crusader army which was led by King Guy of Jerusalem The Crusader army was defeated by the Ayyubid sultan Saladin and Raymond III was captured and taken to Damascus He died on 29 June 1187 while in captivity His death marked the end of the County of Tripoli as an independent state It was later incorporated into the Kingdom of Jerusalem\n\nIn conclusion Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a notable figure in the Crusader states in the Levant He was the count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 He had a close relationship with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and its rulers He is best known for his role in the Battle of Hattin which led to the end of the County of Tripoli as an independent state.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -3.9219088648678735e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001627074379939586}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.3794739544391632}, {"token": "of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -8.940656698541716e-06}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "also", "logprob": -0.515670657157898}, {"token": "known", "logprob": -0.0033385988790541887}, {"token": "as", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -1.549708758830093e-05}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -1.4515655040740967}, {"token": "Ant", "logprob": -0.05276595056056976}, {"token": "io", "logprob": -0.0028166405390948057}, {"token": "ch", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.008056879043579102}, {"token": "a", "logprob": -0.49039262533187866}, {"token": "noble", "logprob": -0.14442910254001617}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.046977631747722626}, {"token": "from", "logprob": -0.46657663583755493}, {"token": "the", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": "Cr", "logprob": -0.7129759788513184}, {"token": "us", "logprob": -2.145764938177308e-06}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -2.753696753643453e-05}, {"token": "states", "logprob": -0.008848031051456928}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -0.03609664365649223}, {"token": "the", "logprob": -4.291525328881107e-06}, {"token": "Lev", "logprob": -0.027360014617443085}, {"token": "ant", "logprob": -3.135155202471651e-05}, {"token": "He", "